// This program is missing a semicolon:
//
//     System.out.println("Hi")
//
// and javac says:
//
//     Main.java:3: error: ';' expected
//             System.out.println("Hi")
//                                     ^
//     1 error
//
// Line 3, and the ^ points at the exact spot. Add the ; and it compiles.

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Hi");
    }
}

// A program that compiles, runs, and then breaks:
//
//     Starting up.
//     Exception in thread "main" java.lang.ArithmeticException: / by zero
//             at Main.main(Main.java:4)
//
// "Starting up." printed, so line 3 worked. Line 4 is the problem:
// dividing by zero is something Java cannot do.

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Starting up.");
        System.out.println(10 / 0);
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Run this as it is: it works.
        // Then break it on purpose, one change at a time, and read each error:
